  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 236 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nNot much changes by the time we get to Sunday with just a gradual \nweakening of the mid-level ridge forecast to continue. This will \nsteadily shift the focus area of the more widespread showers from IA \ninto western MO. Similar to Friday and Saturday, low-level dry air \nwill keep these showers mostly across central MO, with some isolated \nlight showers making it into eastern MO and IL. Instability slightly \nincreases across central MO with 50% probabilities for MUCAPE >200 \nJ/kg by Sunday afternoon according to the LREF. By Monday and \nTuesday, a longwave trough will be traversing into the Desert \nSouthwest, working to place the region into southwesterly flow. \nGuidance has trended considerably slower with the evolution of this \nfeature, more than likely due to model consensus indicating a more \namplified trough now with a closed mid-level low. Now, the general \nconsensus reveals it entering the Great Plains Wednesday into \nThursday, about 12+ hours slower. Moisture and temperature advection \nahead of this system should aid in steadily increasing highs next \nweek along with increasing chances for a more widespread and \npotentially substantial rainfall. Probabilities for 24hr \nprecipitation >0.50\" on the LREF have slightly lowered, but are \nstill around 30-40% for portions of the area. Currently, it appears \nthe best chances for showers and thunderstorms will be Wednesday \ninto Thursday, but with a closed low at play, additional changes in \nthe timing are almost a guarantee. LREF temperature IQRs remain \naround 5-10 degree spreads next week, centered on values slightly \nabove normal. By late next week into the weekend, IQR spread \nincreases, indicating more variance in guidance along with a \npotential cold front behind the longwave trough next week.",
